{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785443201,"nonce":"sz15UNbhUV7VPJmWAgpPfuXSW7A=","message":"**Partially landed 2026-07-31 at commit `fdc7b3a` — the Depot-side half. Read this before continuing; the ticket as written cannot be completed as one slice.**\n\n**Why it split.** `TieredCas` has two instances with different topologies, and only one of them is what ADR-0064 describes:\n\n- **Depot** (`workspaced.rs:401`), warm = a local directory. Warm-first here is the ADR's \"one walk, no network\" literally. **Done.**\n- **Control plane** (`main.rs:216`), warm = `WorkspaceClient` over HTTP to the Depot. **Not done, and it is a decision, not a task.** Warm-first there is still a round trip per object, and it makes warm authoritative on an instance that deliberately opted into `fall_through_on_warm_error()` (`tiered.rs:196-212`) so a Depot outage could not make things wrong. After the change, a Depot outage fails every workspace Step — and not cleanly: the error exits `poll` before `settled_state` is consulted, so the Pod is held for the whole step budget and then fails `Infra{never_started:false}`, which is at-least-once retry of a side-effecting Step. ADR-0064 contains no occurrence of \"outage\" or \"control plane\"; it reasons only about the Depot-side writer.\n\n**Also blocking the control-plane half:** `prune_tree` writes tree objects *after* `ingest` returns (`crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:699-719`), and it is `pruned`, not `snapshot.root`, that gets stamped on the Pod and lands in `attempts.output_snapshot`. Any Step with `outputs:` — the dogfood pipeline included — would publish a root whose trees were never in the flush batch, and the GC's dangling-root self-heal then NULLs the evidence pointer (`retention.rs:232-239`). A batched flush on that path needs an explicit barrier after `prune_tree`, not inside `ingest`.\n\nMost of the control-plane leg is retired by `212bb13` (server exchanges root hashes only) rather than fixed here. **Recommendation: do `212bb13` and re-evaluate, instead of taking the availability regression.**\n\n**Criteria status:**\n\n- [x] Warm-first, single walk, one batched flush gating the settle response — **Depot path**\n- [x] A partial flush never reports success; the batch is idempotent (content-addressed keys; a retry re-offers the whole batch, no cursor)\n- [x] `SettledExportDto::durable` computed from the flush instead of a hard-coded `true`\n- [x] `tiered.rs:250`'s comment rewritten — and the rest of that file's write-path prose with it\n- [x] The required test: kill cold, the settle does not report durable\n- [x] The hand-rolled two-walk `ingest` and its root-disagreement tripwire in `workspaced.rs` deleted; `put_tree`'s canonicalisation-skew tripwire kept\n- [ ] **`TieredCas::ingest`'s own two walks** — still there, still on the live path for every non-Export Step. Blocked on the decision above\n- [ ] **A flush failure names the cause on the Attempt** — filed as `4cf03d7`; unmet on the control-plane path for reasons that predate this slice\n\n**Two omission bugs were found in review and fixed, both the same shape** — worth knowing because a third instance is plausible:\n\n1. The inventory omitted blobs the fold **reused** from the baseline. Cold's GC deletes from cold only while warm has no eviction at all, so warm routinely outlives cold; after a parent aged past retention, a flush skipping reused blobs would publish a cold tree with absent children **and report success**.\n2. Then the identical defect one level up: a rebuilt tree **names** untouched sub-trees, and those tree hashes were in neither list. Fixed by keeping the addresses `content_identity`'s walk already visits — zero extra I/O. Depth is recorded *relative* and shifted per occurrence, because one sub-tree can be named at several depths and a wrong depth silently breaks deepest-first.\n\nReads and writes are now deliberately different handles (`DrainCas` writes warm, reads through the tiered handle). Passing the fold `cas.warm()` alone had removed the cold fall-through, turning a by-design warm-lacks/cold-has state into a hard 500 where it used to self-heal.\n\n**Filed from this work:** `38b945e` (the flush re-reads and re-hashes every blob before asking cold — the cost this slice traded for correctness), `d4d3b95` (the GC mark walk reads warm, so a torn cold tier marks clean — which is why these bugs would have been invisible in production), `483f583` (the skew tripwire is unreachable as written), `4cf03d7` (a persistent drain failure presents as a timeout).\n\n925 tests pass, 26 skipped. Nine new; five mutations re-applied independently and all five killed their test.","files":null}]}